1
00:00:00,433 --> 00:00:02,454
And what you typically would do when

2
00:00:02,518 --> 00:00:05,246
building a backend application where you're

3
00:00:05,310 --> 00:00:07,829
handling a POST request that might be

4
00:00:07,893 --> 00:00:10,509
received because a form was submitted

5
00:00:11,407 --> 00:00:14,247
is you would use the response object to

6
00:00:14,552 --> 00:00:18,772
issue a redirect, to send the user along to

7
00:00:18,964 --> 00:00:20,119
a different route.

8
00:00:20,970 --> 00:00:23,312
And then the browser would load that route

9
00:00:23,312 --> 00:00:25,671
and the page of that route, and that page

10
00:00:25,783 --> 00:00:28,559
then might include that latest data that was

11
00:00:28,607 --> 00:00:28,703
sent.

12
00:00:30,139 --> 00:00:33,075
So here we might redirect to just /nothing,

13
00:00:33,285 --> 00:00:36,948
so to the root page of this site, which is

14
00:00:37,545 --> 00:00:40,580
in the end this route here so that this page

15
00:00:40,677 --> 00:00:43,082
is re-rendered. And since we're outputting

16
00:00:43,179 --> 00:00:44,164
all the list items here,

17
00:00:44,713 --> 00:00:47,426
our new list item should be included there

18
00:00:48,105 --> 00:00:49,157
after we stored it.

19
00:00:50,775 --> 00:00:52,490
That's the theory at least.

20
00:00:53,218 --> 00:00:56,152
And therefore now if we save everything, and

21
00:00:56,185 --> 00:00:57,471
I reload this, if I

22
00:00:58,018 --> 00:01:00,928
enter test here and I click Save Note,

23
00:01:02,521 --> 00:01:02,730
okay,

24
00:01:03,727 --> 00:01:05,029
I get this weird result.

25
00:01:06,570 --> 00:01:06,715
My

26
00:01:07,409 --> 00:01:10,103
look here changed quite a bit. Suddenly all

27
00:01:10,152 --> 00:01:12,588
the content is very much in the middle, but

28
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:16,122
this item has actually been added. I can see

29
00:01:16,122 --> 00:01:16,448
it here,

30
00:01:17,248 --> 00:01:19,141
but of course all that content is now

31
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:21,115
suddenly rendered in the wrong place.

32
00:01:22,655 --> 00:01:24,837
And actually, if you take a closer look,

33
00:01:25,383 --> 00:01:28,159
it looks like the page that was served

34
00:01:28,191 --> 00:01:28,769
before

35
00:01:29,459 --> 00:01:29,812
is now

36
00:01:30,341 --> 00:01:33,133
rendered again, just in a different place.

37
00:01:34,208 --> 00:01:37,835
For example, this sentence here is also

38
00:01:37,963 --> 00:01:38,669
rendered again.

39
00:01:39,824 --> 00:01:41,830
And if I scroll down quite a bit,

40
00:01:42,536 --> 00:01:44,446
I have the header here again, and then I

41
00:01:44,462 --> 00:01:46,259
have the list again, but now

42
00:01:47,029 --> 00:01:48,939
without that new item I added.

43
00:01:50,432 --> 00:01:52,744
So what's happening here?

44
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:57,191
Now, what's happening here is that HTMX

45
00:01:57,560 --> 00:01:59,872
sends that request, that

46
00:02:00,531 --> 00:02:01,815
POST request here,

47
00:02:02,843 --> 00:02:03,630
but it then

48
00:02:04,288 --> 00:02:07,531
actually also renders the response

49
00:02:08,061 --> 00:02:10,646
in the element that sent the request.

50
00:02:11,449 --> 00:02:14,820
That's what you learned before with hx-get,

51
00:02:15,206 --> 00:02:18,929
and it's the same for hx-post. And that's

52
00:02:19,538 --> 00:02:22,055
really important to understand and keep in

53
00:02:22,055 --> 00:02:24,540
mind. With HTMX, you

54
00:02:25,165 --> 00:02:28,211
do not send requests and then it's the

55
00:02:28,211 --> 00:02:30,776
backend that redirects the user or anything

56
00:02:30,776 --> 00:02:33,549
like that. But instead, the idea is that you

57
00:02:33,597 --> 00:02:35,825
send behind-the-scenes requests

58
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:39,191
where you then also handle the response that

59
00:02:39,191 --> 00:02:40,233
is sent back

60
00:02:40,875 --> 00:02:42,269
behind the scenes, so to say.

61
00:02:43,503 --> 00:02:45,764
That is what we did with hx-get.

62
00:02:46,469 --> 00:02:50,172
We used HTMX to send a GET request and The

63
00:02:50,317 --> 00:02:53,352
data that was sent back, that list,

64
00:02:53,946 --> 00:02:57,655
was then rendered in a place of our choice

65
00:02:57,864 --> 00:02:59,212
on the existing page.

66
00:03:00,047 --> 00:03:01,862
And it's now the same thing here with this

67
00:03:01,974 --> 00:03:04,543
POST request for this form. We send a POST

68
00:03:04,543 --> 00:03:07,385
request and the data that's sent back is

69
00:03:07,514 --> 00:03:09,569
then rendered inside of that form because

70
00:03:09,665 --> 00:03:12,106
that's the default behavior of HTMX.

71
00:03:12,652 --> 00:03:16,295
If you did not specify hx-swap, and/or

72
00:03:16,488 --> 00:03:19,695
hx-target to change where and how the

73
00:03:19,695 --> 00:03:21,924
response is rendered, it will be rendered

74
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:24,217
inside the element that sent the request.

75
00:03:25,083 --> 00:03:27,216
And that's why the form was actually

76
00:03:27,232 --> 00:03:29,701
replaced with the page content again, with

77
00:03:29,733 --> 00:03:31,128
the entire page content.

78
00:03:32,059 --> 00:03:33,213
That's what we see here.

79
00:03:34,143 --> 00:03:35,218
Now you might say,

80
00:03:36,067 --> 00:03:37,463
but we did redirect here.

81
00:03:38,377 --> 00:03:38,505
Why

82
00:03:39,114 --> 00:03:41,888
does HTMX render some response?

83
00:03:42,626 --> 00:03:46,655
Well, because HTMX follows that redirect.

84
00:03:47,682 --> 00:03:48,854
If I redirect here,

85
00:03:49,609 --> 00:03:50,235
what happens?

86
00:03:51,294 --> 00:03:54,520
This first route here is triggered and that

87
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:57,329
route returns some HTML code and that's

88
00:03:57,474 --> 00:04:02,016
ultimately therefore the final response HTMX

89
00:04:02,064 --> 00:04:03,316
receives and renders.

90
00:04:04,231 --> 00:04:07,169
That's why this page was now rendered again

91
00:04:07,747 --> 00:04:10,090
into the element that sent the request.

92
00:04:11,598 --> 00:04:14,789
That's how HTMX works. And therefore, when

93
00:04:14,902 --> 00:04:18,799
using HTMX, you have to think differently

94
00:04:18,831 --> 00:04:20,563
about returning responses.

95
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:23,850
And that's why it makes a lot of sense to

96
00:04:23,866 --> 00:04:27,058
also own the backend code so that you can

97
00:04:27,090 --> 00:04:30,506
make sure that you are sending responses

98
00:04:31,259 --> 00:04:35,237
that are useful to HTMX and that can be used

99
00:04:35,349 --> 00:04:36,295
by HTMX.

100
00:04:36,969 --> 00:04:38,816
For example, what we could do here

101
00:04:39,345 --> 00:04:40,935
is instead of redirecting,

102
00:04:41,529 --> 00:04:44,227
which we typically would do in a traditional

103
00:04:44,484 --> 00:04:45,640
full-stack application,

104
00:04:46,330 --> 00:04:47,711
we can send back the

105
00:04:48,578 --> 00:04:51,532
HTML code of the updated list, for example.

106
00:04:51,902 --> 00:04:53,523
So I could grab this code here

107
00:04:55,017 --> 00:04:58,437
and then send back an HTML fragment in the

108
00:04:58,453 --> 00:04:58,678
end

109
00:04:59,336 --> 00:05:01,407
that includes that updated list.

110
00:05:02,905 --> 00:05:05,632
So I'm not sending back the entire page, I'm

111
00:05:05,632 --> 00:05:08,163
not redirecting, I'm just sending back the

112
00:05:08,163 --> 00:05:09,997
content that needs to be updated.

113
00:05:11,630 --> 00:05:13,405
Now, just like that, it of course still

114
00:05:13,437 --> 00:05:16,149
wouldn't work. If I reload and I add a brand

115
00:05:16,166 --> 00:05:16,714
new note,

116
00:05:17,247 --> 00:05:19,798
now it would be that list fragment that

117
00:05:19,798 --> 00:05:22,397
replaces the form and the old list would

118
00:05:22,479 --> 00:05:25,478
then still be there below that form that was

119
00:05:25,576 --> 00:05:26,408
now replaced.

120
00:05:27,149 --> 00:05:29,614
And this behavior of course makes sense

121
00:05:29,614 --> 00:05:31,377
because as I mentioned, the content that is

122
00:05:31,377 --> 00:05:34,064
received is rendered into the element that

123
00:05:34,064 --> 00:05:36,622
is responsible for sending the request, so

124
00:05:36,655 --> 00:05:37,568
into the form here.

125
00:05:38,930 --> 00:05:40,443
Therefore, what we should do now

126
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:43,148
is set a different target,

127
00:05:43,937 --> 00:05:46,433
the unordered list specifically, so that we

128
00:05:46,433 --> 00:05:50,131
target this unordered list with hx-target.

129
00:05:51,885 --> 00:05:54,393
And then we also add hx-swap

130
00:05:54,942 --> 00:05:55,137
to

131
00:05:55,767 --> 00:05:59,601
replace the entire list because I know that

132
00:05:59,601 --> 00:06:02,546
I'll be getting back an entirely new list

133
00:06:02,982 --> 00:06:05,000
due to the way I wrote this code here.

134
00:06:05,969 --> 00:06:08,343
And therefore it's the entire old list that

135
00:06:08,375 --> 00:06:09,425
should be replaced.

136
00:06:10,363 --> 00:06:13,452
And that can be achieved by setting hx-swap

137
00:06:13,452 --> 00:06:14,589
to outerHTML.

138
00:06:16,503 --> 00:06:18,616
That will replace not just the content

139
00:06:18,745 --> 00:06:21,858
inside the target, but instead the entire

140
00:06:21,987 --> 00:06:24,390
target with the response content.

141
00:06:26,410 --> 00:06:28,491
And with that, if I save this,

142
00:06:29,178 --> 00:06:31,744
So now that we added these 3

143
00:06:32,434 --> 00:06:33,765
attributes, if we save this,

144
00:06:34,438 --> 00:06:36,715
and now that we sent back that fragment,

145
00:06:37,565 --> 00:06:39,618
if I reload and I enter test again,

146
00:06:40,147 --> 00:06:43,658
now it's just the list that was updated and

147
00:06:43,658 --> 00:06:45,887
it is now updated every time I send a new

148
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:46,641
request.

149
00:06:47,523 --> 00:06:50,554
And that's now, for example, the behavior we

150
00:06:50,554 --> 00:06:51,051
want here.
